     {
      "slug": "lesson-90f614241c87",
      "topic": "tailscale acl basics",
      "created": false,
      "content": "<p><strong>ACL rules</strong> specify which <strong>source</strong> nodes can reach which <strong>destination</strong> nodes and ports. They are written as JSON objects in the <code>acl</code> array of the <code>tailscale.com</code> config.</p>\n<p><strong>Tags</strong> are labels you attach to devices instead of users. A tag turns a device into an identity that can be referenced in ACL rules, allowing the rule to apply to all devices with that tag regardless of who owns them.</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre><code>{\n  \"action\": \"accept\",\n  \"src\": [\"group:admins\"],\n  \"dst\": [\"tag:server\"],\n  \"ports\": [\"22\"]\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Here <code>group:admins</code> is a user group, while <code>tag:server</code> refers to any device marked with the <code>server</code> tag. The rule permits admins to SSH into any tagged server.</p>\n<p>Key differences:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>ACL rule</strong> defines the traffic policy (who can talk to whom on which ports).</li>\n<li><strong>Tag</strong> is a reusable identity that can be applied to multiple devices, decoupling access control from individual users.</li>\n<li>Using tags simplifies management when devices change ownership or when you need to grant or revoke access for a whole class of machines.</li>\n</ul>",
      "next_review": "2026-10-31",
      "status": "open"
     }
    ],
    "tool_calls": 3
   },
