     {
      "slug": "lesson-80a7b20bd1e4",
      "topic": "CAP theorem in distributed systems",
      "created": true,
      "content": "<p><strong>CAP theorem states that a distributed data store can guarantee at most two of consistency, availability, and partition tolerance.</strong> When a network partition occurs, you must choose between maintaining consistency (CP) or availability (AP), while partition tolerance is always required.</p><p>In practice:</p><ul><li><strong>CP (Consistency + Partition tolerance)</strong>: The system refuses writes or delays responses during a partition to keep data consistent.</li><li><strong>AP (Availability + Partition tolerance)</strong>: The system continues to serve requests, accepting that data may become temporarily inconsistent and will be reconciled later.</li></ul><p>When no partition exists, a system can provide both consistency and availability. The CAP theorem guides design decisions for distributed databases, messaging systems, and replicated services.</p>",
      "next_review": "2026-10-31",
      "status": "open"
     }
    ],
    "tool_calls": 1
   },
