      "slug": "lesson-098b5e999880",
      "topic": "LLM quantization",
      "created": true,
      "content": "<h3>LLM Quantization</h3><p>Quantization reduces the precision of model weights from 16‑ or 32‑bit floating point to 8‑, 4‑bit, or lower integer representations, shrinking the model size and speeding up inference at the cost of a small drop in accuracy.</p><p>GGUF (Generalized Graph Unified Format) is a file format that stores these quantized weights along with a memory‑friendly layout, enabling fast mapping into RAM and efficient local inference. Because the weights are smaller and the layout is optimized for quick access, GGUF models run noticeably faster than their full‑precision counterparts.</p>",
      "next_review": "2026-10-31",
      "status": "open"
     }
